--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertContains` finds an object by identity, so a twin holding the same fields is not in the array and an alias of an entry is — the row that parts it from a field comparison — `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`, `rule:expressions/equality-semantics`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `Core\Arr::contains`'s comparison answers the object row by identity: two
// objects are the same value when they are the same allocation and never
// because their properties agree. `assertEquals` is the member that names a
// comparison of contents, and it names it at the call site -- so a membership
// question that quietly walked fields would be the silent fallback `rule:classes/comparable`
// refused for ordering, arrived at through a different member.
//
// A twin and an alias are the two sides of that: same fields, different
// allocation, and same allocation reached through a second name.

final class Point {
    public function constructor(public int $x) {}
}

final class Ask {
    public static function verdict(array<Point> $subject, Point $needle): string {
        try {
            Core\Test::assertContains($subject, $needle);
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $byIdentity) {
            return "failed";
        }
        return "held";
    }
}

var $first = new Point(1);
var $second = new Point(2);
var $twin = new Point(1);
var $alias = $first;

array<Point> $points = [$first, $second];

// 1. Both sides of the bound: the entry itself is found, a second name for it
// is the same value, and a twin with identical fields is not in the array.
echo Ask::verdict($points, $first), " ", Ask::verdict($points, $alias), " ",
     Ask::verdict($points, $twin), "\n";

// 2. Identity is the allocation and not the field, so mutating the entry does
// not lose it and mutating the twin into agreement does not find it.
$first->x = 9;
echo Ask::verdict($points, $first), " ", Ask::verdict($points, $twin), "\n";
$twin->x = 9;
echo Ask::verdict($points, $twin), "\n";

// 3. The array is what is searched, so appending the twin is what puts it in
// -- the one operation that changes the answer.
$points[] = $twin;
echo Ask::verdict($points, $twin), " ", Ask::verdict($points, new Point(9)), "\n";

// 4. An empty roster of the same class holds none of them.
array<Point> $none = [];
echo Ask::verdict($none, $first), "\n";
--EXPECT--
held held failed
held failed
failed
held failed
failed
